## TIER 4 — Sensitivities, greeks & hedge ratios (how to hedge it)

Turns the valuation into hedge instructions.

**4.1 Tornado / sensitivity bars.**
*Type:* horizontal tornado of ∂(margin) to a defined bump in each driver: **forward power level**, **gas price**, **spike vol / cap-implied vol**, **power–gas correlation**, **MLF**, **start cost**, **forced-outage rate**.
*Shows:* what the valuation is most exposed to, ranked.
*Desk action:* prioritises which exposures to hedge and which to monitor. Usually power level and gas dominate; correlation and MLF are the sneaky ones.

**4.2 Delta curves (power & gas) vs price level.**
*Type:* line — ∂(asset value)/∂(power forward) and ∂/∂(gas forward) as a function of the forward level. Show the **low, rising ATM delta** characteristic of a peaker.
*Shows:* the dynamic hedge ratio — small near current forwards, rising as the asset moves into the money.
*Desk action:* sets the delta hedge and tells the desk how much it will need to **rebalance** as forwards move (the gamma).

**4.3 Cap-equivalent and swap-equivalent replication.**
*Type:* per quarter: the asset's sensitivity expressed as **equivalent short-cap MW** + **equivalent swap MW** that replicate its forward greeks.
*Shows:* "this asset ≈ short `N_eq` caps + `V_eq` swaps."
*Desk action:* **the hedge construction.** The desk hedges the residual after the natural cap-short, in tradeable instruments.

**4.4 Vega to cap-implied vol.**
*Type:* bar per quarter — sensitivity to the implied vol embedded in cap premiums.
*Shows:* the asset is **short vol** (it sold the tail). Quantifies by how much.
*Desk action:* if the desk wants to be vol-neutral, this sizes the offsetting vol position; if it wants the carry, this is the deliberate exposure.

