## TIER 5 — Stress & scenarios (what breaks it)

Deterministic shocks the desk reasons about directly. A grid, not a fan.

**5.1 Scenario impact table / heatmap.**
*Type:* table or heatmap of margin impact (mean and CVaR) for each named scenario:

| Scenario | Why it matters in the NEM |
|---|---|
| Gas spike +$X/GJ | SRMC rises; defence margin compresses |
| **Gas–power co-spike** | SRMC moves against you *exactly when caps pay* — the signature peaker risk |
| Heatwave (spike frequency ×2) | more cap payouts; tests whether you're net long or short the tail |
| Prolonged scarcity → **APC binds ($600)** | sustained high prices truncated; caps-short capped on the upside too |
| Forced outage during a spike | the **uncovered-tail realisation** — pure loss at near-MPC |
| MLF cut | covered-defence leakage `(1−L)·S` widens |
| Low-price / mild summer | premium carry intact, energy spread evaporates |

*Shows:* discrete, explainable failure modes.
*Desk action:* sets stop-losses, informs `N` sizing, and tells the desk which scenarios it is implicitly betting against.

**5.2 Tail decomposition (CVaR by cause).**
*Type:* stacked bar — contribution to CVaR(5%) by scenario type (uncovered spike vs gas blowout vs sustained low prices).
*Shows:* *what* the bad outcomes are made of.
*Desk action:* lets the desk hedge the dominant tail driver specifically rather than buying blanket protection.

**5.3 Co-spike correlation stress.**
*Type:* margin (mean + tail) as a function of the power–gas correlation/co-spike assumption.
*Shows:* sensitivity to the joint-modelling assumption that is hardest to calibrate and most consequential.
*Desk action:* a confidence band on the valuation reflecting correlation uncertainty — and a flag if the trade only works under benign correlation.

